fix(a11y): the last two are the Face's own hues, and latte is where they fail

Third pass at the same mistake, and the last one. Lighthouse in latte still
had `color-contrast`: `--blue` at 4.34:1 on the footer's credit and
`--accent` at 4.45:1 on the current section in the top bar, both link text at
18px, which is not large text and does not get the 3:1 bar. Near misses, and
therefore misses.

Same answer as `--muted`, and cheaper: `--link` and `--current`, the blue and
the accent mixed one fifth of the way toward `--text`. That lands 4.89:1 and
5.34:1 in latte and leaves mocha above 8:1, and they are still recognisably
the blue and the accent. One percentage rather than two, and one rule rather
than one per scheme — mixing toward text works in both directions, where
`--muted` mixes toward base and so needs a block each.

Lighthouse desktop is now 100 for accessibility, best practices, SEO and
agentic browsing in both schemes: 56 audits, none failing, where each scheme
was 96 with one.

docs/evidence/S7.2-accessibility-and-performance.md is the measurement — both
Lighthouse passes, the three contrast rounds with the ratio that failed each
one, the layout-shift trace that reports zero entries across a load and four
flips, and the two things the numbers show that reading would not: that
`--muted` needs a percentage per scheme because latte's text has 7.06:1 of
headroom where mocha's has 11.34:1, and that mantle rather than base is the
binding surface, because latte's mantle is darker than its base and the top
bar and evidence strip both sit on it.
